WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:03.400
Last clip, the loop stopped at one
gate. Now you're standing at it.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.267
Nothing leaves without a second
pair of eyes: every message,

00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:08.067
every budget move and every live change

00:00:08.067 --> 00:00:10.133
waits for a person who didn't propose it.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.733
In this clip you'll read an
approval, work the queue,

00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:15.067
and know exactly who can approve and why.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.133
Sophia approves a send on Monday.
By Tuesday the draft has changed,

00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:21.400
and the message that went
out isn't the one she read.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.800
Here, that can't happen.

00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:25.600
An approval is the record of a
proposal waiting for a person.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.367
Any external effect, like a send,

00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:29.600
and any live configuration
change becomes one.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.033
The rule is four eyes: the proposer,
or the human an agent acts for,

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:35.167
can never approve, sign or execute.

00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:38.333
First, the states, so you know
what's waiting and for how long.

00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:42.200
An approval moves from proposed to
approved, then executing, then executed.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.767
It can also be rejected, withdrawn
by its proposer, revoked, or fail.

00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:47.900
A proposal nobody decides
expires after seven days.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.800
In the console: Approvals, and open
any approval to see its timeline.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.533
Or ask your agent: "What
is waiting for approval,

00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:55.833
and what is the oldest one".

00:00:55.867 --> 00:00:58.733
Second, read the payload. It's
frozen the moment it's proposed,

00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:00.200
and you see a diff of what would change.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.133
Configuration changes
add an impact preview.

00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:04.167
The approval is bound
to the exact payload,

00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:05.567
so what you approve is what runs.

00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:08.600
In the console: Approvals, open
the approval, and read the diff.

00:01:08.633 --> 00:01:11.067
Third, the claims panel,
for outbound content.

00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:13.800
Every factual claim, like a
statistic or a named customer,

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.567
is checked against proof you've approved.

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:17.300
Each one shows as backed,
close, or no proof.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:18.067
Close is never treated as backed.

00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:20.300
By default an unbacked
claim blocks the send.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.767
In the other mode it routes to the
approver, who acknowledges each claim.

00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:25.400
The proposer and the agent can't.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.867
The check runs when the message is
proposed, and again when it's sent.

00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:31.767
If the proof is archived or
expires in between, the run fails.

00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:34.800
Fourth, the receipt. If the
approval ran in dev or staging,

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.767
a real customer facing
effect is recorded, not sent,

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:38.467
and the receipt says not applied, dev.

00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:42.000
Configuration changes do apply in the
environment they were proposed for,

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.167
whatever environment you're viewing.

00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:45.433
Read the receipt before you
tell anyone it went out.

00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:49.467
Last, decide. In the console: Mission
Control shows today's one move,

00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:52.033
the oldest pending approval,
with one click to approve.

00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:55.233
Or open Approvals, read the
payload, the claims and the impact,

00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:55.833
and approve or reject.

00:01:55.833 --> 00:01:59.233
Each decision is attributed to
you and kept in the history.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.533
Every approval records who proposed
it, which agent was involved,

00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:05.333
which human that agent
acted for, and who approved.

00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:09.533
Months later, you can answer who let
this happen, and why, without guessing.

00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:10.767
Not everything waiting is a send.

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:14.667
Needs a human is the queue for exactly
what waits on a person right now:

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:17.100
effect approvals, configuration
decisions with an impact preview,

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.967
and entity resolution reviews. In
the console: Operate, Needs a human.

00:02:20.967 --> 00:02:23.500
Or ask your agent: "Show me
everything that needs a human".

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.300
Advanced move: clear it once in the
morning and once before you leave,

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.367
so the seven day expiry
never catches anything.

00:02:28.433 --> 00:02:31.300
Open asks cover the rest:
questions, document requests,

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.700
access requests and data gaps.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.867
Each is numbered, with
an owner and a due date,

00:02:34.867 --> 00:02:37.500
and GTM OS chases the owner
every day until it's answered.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.467
In the console: Operate, Open asks.

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:42.500
Or ask your agent: "Which open asks
are mine, and which are overdue".

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.467
Agents open asks too, so a missing
answer becomes a visible item,

00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:46.567
not a silent guess.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.800
So who can approve? Agents never can.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.867
The person needs a role
with the approve permission,

00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:52.067
which is owner or admin.

00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:55.133
And they can't be the proposer,
or the human an agent acts for.

00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:56.700
One rule closes a loophole.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.233
The person who created an
agent key can't approve,

00:02:59.233 --> 00:03:01.400
reject or execute that agent's proposals.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.000
Executing an approved
action is human only too,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.533
and it needs a second person.

00:03:05.567 --> 00:03:07.700
So when you ask an agent
to approve something,

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.700
it stops and names the
screen and the button.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.600
That's the system working, not failing.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.267
On a one person team, name a second
identity as approver. That's by design.

00:03:15.333 --> 00:03:18.400
The worry: a second approver on
everything will slow us down.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.700
Three things keep it fast.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.300
Mission Control puts the
oldest approval one click away.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.833
A person can grant an agent an
envelope for a narrow scope,

00:03:24.833 --> 00:03:28.333
with a budget and an expiry. And the
queue shows only what needs a person.

00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:31.833
Run it back: The payload is frozen,
the claims are checked twice,

00:03:31.833 --> 00:03:35.000
and the person who approves is
never the person who proposed.

00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:36.533
You always know what you're approving,

00:03:36.533 --> 00:03:40.000
and why nobody approves their own
work. Read one approval timeline today.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.600
After this comes Dry Mode and guardrails,
which sets how fast you go live.
